# Final whole-branch review fixes

## Context

The whole-branch review of the Lakeflow-only analytics work found two things the
eleven per-task reviews could not, because each is a seam between tasks rather
than a defect inside one.

The first is a silent wrong-number bug. Before this work, the app always passed
`exclude_predicates=None`, so the job fell back to its own defaults —
`rdf:type`, `rdfs:label`, `rdfs:comment`, `rdfs:seeAlso`, the predicates that
never form an entity-entity edge. Task 6 started forwarding
`MetricsRequest.predicate_filter`, and the job treated a non-empty list as the
complete set rather than as an addition. So a user excluding one noisy predicate
would promote `rdf:type` into an edge, wiring every instance to its class node
and inflating every degree and PageRank in the run. Nothing would fail; the
numbers would just be wrong.

The second is stale operator-facing copy in `app.yaml.template`, which still
described the pre-change world: a triple cap that switched between NetworkX and
engine-side SQL, Neo4j and `app_managed` Lakebase as unsupported, and the claim
that turning the toggle on is "safe even if the prerequisites are not met"
because a run degrades rather than failing. All three are now the opposite of
the truth, and an operator reads this file to decide how to configure a deploy.

## Changes

1. `src/jobs/graph_analytics_job.py` — Extracted
   `merge_excluded_predicates(raw)`, which unions the caller's
   `--exclude-predicates` with `DEFAULT_EXCLUDED_PREDICATES` and de-duplicates,
   and called it from `main()`. Pulling it out of `main()` also makes it testable:
   `main()` needs a live `SparkSession`, so the merge had no unit coverage where
   it sat.
2. `tests/units/core/test_graph_analytics_job_exit.py` — Four tests: no filter
   yields exactly the defaults, a caller predicate is added to rather than
   replacing them, naming a default does not duplicate it, and blank entries are
   ignored.
3. `app.yaml.template` — Rewrote the three analytics comment blocks for the
   single path: the triple cap belongs to community detection, the job computes
   every KPI and is the only thing that does, and every backend behaves
   identically because the job reads the snapshot each Build materialises.

## Modified Files

- `src/jobs/graph_analytics_job.py`
- `tests/units/core/test_graph_analytics_job_exit.py`
- `app.yaml.template`

## Test Result

`pytest -q -m "not scenario"` → **3764 passed, 275 skipped, 1 xfailed, 0 failed**
